<DocsLayout title="Running Your First Task — Band Docs">
  <h1>Running Your First Task</h1>

  <p>
    A "task" in Band is a message you send to a coding agent in one of a worktree's
    chat panes. When you send the message, Band dispatches it to the agent, which works
    inside the worktree's git worktree. You can watch the agent's progress in real time
    from the dashboard or your terminal.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>

  <p>Before sending your first message, make sure you have:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>A <a href="/docs/first-repo">registered repo</a> in Band.</li>
    <li>A <a href="/docs/first-worktree">worktree</a> created for that repo.</li>
    <li>A coding agent (such as Claude Code, Codex, or OpenCode) installed and detected by Band.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Send a message via the dashboard</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>Open the Band dashboard and select a worktree from the sidebar.</li>
    <li>In the worktree view, focus the chat panel.</li>
    <li>Type your prompt describing what you want the agent to do.</li>
    <li>Press <strong>Enter</strong> (or click <strong>Send</strong>) to dispatch the message.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Band lazy-creates a "Chat" pane the first time a worktree receives a message, then
    reuses it for follow-ups. The agent's output streams into the pane in real time.
  </p>

  <h2>Send a message via the CLI</h2>

  <p>
    To submit a prompt from your terminal, change into the worktree directory and use
    <code>band chats send</code>. The worktree and chat pane are auto-detected from
    your current working directory:
  </p>

  <pre><code>cd ~/.band/worktrees/my-app/feat-validation
band chats send --message "Add input validation to all API endpoints"</code></pre>

  <p>
    If you're not inside the worktree's directory, pass the worktree ID explicitly:
  </p>

  <pre><code>band chats send --worktree ws_abc123 --message "Add input validation to all API endpoints"</code></pre>

  <p>
    A common one-shot pattern is to create a worktree and kick off the agent in a
    single command. <code>band worktrees create</code> accepts a <code>--prompt</code>
    flag that creates the worktree, registers the worktree, and queues the message in
    its first chat pane:
  </p>

  <pre><code>band worktrees create my-app feat/validation \
  --prompt "Add input validation to all API endpoints"</code></pre>

  <h2>Chat status</h2>

  <p>Every chat pane goes through these states (visible as colored dots in the sidebar):</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Waiting</strong> &mdash; The chat is idle. No task is running.</li>
    <li><strong>Running</strong> &mdash; The agent is actively working on a message.</li>
    <li><strong>Completed</strong> &mdash; The last task finished successfully.</li>
    <li><strong>Failed</strong> &mdash; The last task ended with an unrecoverable error.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Claude Code chats also display <em>needs attention</em> when the agent calls
    <code>AskUserQuestion</code> or <code>ExitPlanMode</code>. See
    <a href="/docs/agent-status">Agent Status</a> for the full hook flow.
  </p>

  <h2>Watching progress</h2>

  <p>
    The dashboard shows chat output in real time. From the CLI, you can stream the same
    NDJSON event feed with <code>band chats watch</code>:
  </p>

  <pre><code># Stream the cwd worktree's first chat pane
band chats watch

# Filter for text deltas only
band chats watch | jq -r 'select(.type == "text-delta") | .delta'

# Target a specific chat pane
band chats watch chat_abc</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>band chats watch</code> exits immediately if no task is running, so it's safe
    to invoke right after <code>band chats send</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Managing chats</h2>

  <pre><code>band chats list                       # chats in the cwd worktree
band chats list --output json | \
  jq '.chats[] | select(.status == "running")'   # filter by status
band chats stop                       # abort the current task in the active chat
band chats remove                     # delete the chat pane (kills the agent)</code></pre>

  <h2>Running multiple tasks in parallel</h2>

  <p>
    Because each worktree is an isolated git worktree, you can run multiple agents
    across different worktrees simultaneously without file conflicts:
  </p>

  <pre><code>band worktrees create my-app feat/auth  --prompt "Implement OAuth2 login flow"
band worktrees create my-app feat/api   --prompt "Add pagination to all list endpoints"
band worktrees create my-app fix/tests  --prompt "Fix the 12 failing integration tests"</code></pre>

  <p>
    You now know the core Band workflow: register a repo, create a worktree, and
    chat with an agent inside it. From here you can explore repo configuration with
    <code>.band/config.json</code>, remote tunnels, and cronjobs.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
